      {
        "id": "chapter-review",
        "title": "Chapter Review",
        "advances": null,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The family of relative constructions is wider than the name *relative clause* suggests. It includes wh, that and bare clauses, fused noun phrases and prepositional phrases, and the relatives that appear in clefts. What unites them is a link between a clause-internal position and an antecedent, whether that antecedent is a separate phrase or folded into the relative word itself."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Relational labels describe where the clause sits and what it does there. Integrated relatives modify a nominal and typically restrict it; supplementary relatives are detached and add information about a wider range of anchors; cleft relatives belong to the cleft construction; fused relatives combine antecedent and clause in a single phrase. Infinitival relatives are hollow clauses whose missing element comes from the head."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The mechanics are consistent across the types. A relative phrase can be simple or complex, with the relative feature percolating upward from the word to the larger phrase; relativisation can target subjects, objects, genitives, adjuncts and deeply embedded positions; and the gap always preserves the grammatical function of the element the antecedent interprets."
          },
          {
            "type": "note",
            "tone": "why",
            "title": "A relative-construction checklist",
            "text": "Find the antecedent and the relative phrase. Identify the gap and its function. Decide whether the relative is integrated or supplementary, and whether it is wh, that or bare. Then check for fused, cleft or infinitival structure."
          }
        ],
        "check": {
          "prompt": "Which statement correctly describes the gap in a relative clause?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "a",
              "text": "It is an abstract position co-indexed with the antecedent or relative phrase.",
              "feedback": "The gap preserves the function of the relativised element."
            },
            {
              "id": "b",
              "text": "It is always filled by that.",
              "feedback": "That is a subordinator, not the gap."
            },
            {
              "id": "c",
              "text": "It only occurs in subject position.",
              "feedback": "Gaps can be objects, complements, adjuncts and embedded elements."
            }
          ],
          "answer": "a"
        }
      }
